  "Full API spec": "Import graph and ownership: The project import graph is strictly acyclic and one-way: excel_processor/model.py defines Cell, Row, and Sheet and imports no project modules. excel_processor/operations.py, excel_processor/csv_io.py, and excel_processor/report.py may import only from .model. excel_processor/__init__.py imports from those four modules only to re-export canonical public names. Main.py and tests/test_main.py import from excel_processor; they do not import lower modules directly. No module imports __init__, Main, or another operation module, and no wildcard imports are allowed.\n\nexcel_processor.model:\n- class Cell:\n  - signature: Cell(value: object | None = None)\n  - attributes: value: object | None (mutable)\n  - semantics: simple container for a single cell value\n- class Row:\n  - signature: Row(cells: list[Cell])\n  - behavior: defensively copies the input list of Cell; does not share the list reference\n  - methods:\n    - values(self) -> list[object]: returns a snapshot list of each contained Cell.value in order; the returned list is a new list and further cell mutations do not alter the snapshot\n- class Sheet:\n  - signature: Sheet(rows: list[Row])\n  - behavior: defensively copies the input list of Row; internal structure is private\n  - classmethods:\n    - from_values(values: list[list[object]]) -> Sheet: constructs a new Sheet by creating new Row and Cell objects. Rejects ragged non-empty rows with ValueError: among rows that have non-zero length, all must share equal length. Empty sheet is allowed; sheet with zero rows has column_count() == 0.\n  - methods:\n    - to_values(self) -> list[list[object]]: returns a deep snapshot of current values (primitive values only), not sharing internal lists\n    - row_count(self) -> int: number of rows\n    - column_count(self) -> int: number of columns; 0 when there are no rows\n    - get_cell(self, row: int, column: int) -> Cell: validates non-negative indices and raises IndexError if out-of-range\n    - set_cell(self, row: int, column: int, value: object) -> None: validates indices and sets the cell value; raises IndexError if out-of-range\n\nexcel_processor.operations (imports only from .model):\n- filter_rows(sheet: Sheet, predicate: Callable[[Row], bool]) -> Sheet:\n  - produces a new Sheet containing only rows where predicate(row) is True\n  - stable order (preserves original ordering of kept rows)\n  - returns a Sheet with entirely new Row and Cell instances; does not mutate input\n- sort_rows(sheet: Sheet, key: Callable[[Row], object], reverse: bool = False) -> Sheet:\n  - returns a new Sheet sorted stably by key(row); reverse flag controls order\n  - produces entirely new Row and Cell objects; input untouched\n- aggregate_column(sheet: Sheet, column: int) -> dict[str, object]:\n  - validates that column is within [0, sheet.column_count() - 1]; raises IndexError otherwise\n  - computes and returns a dict with exactly keys: \"count\" (total row count), \"missing\" (count of None or \"\" values in the target column), \"numeric_count\" (count of numeric values of type int or float, excluding bool), \"sum\" (sum of numeric values or 0 when there are none), \"min\" (numeric min or None if none), \"max\" (numeric max or None if none)\n  - values are derived solely from the specified column\n\nexcel_processor.csv_io (imports only from .model; uses csv and io.StringIO; no filesystem access):\n- loads_csv(text: str) -> Sheet:\n  - parses CSV from the provided text using io.StringIO and csv.reader\n  - every cell value is kept as a string; empty CSV cells remain \"\"\n  - detects ragged rows relative to the first row length; raises ValueError on ragged input\n  - returns a Sheet constructed via Sheet.from_values with string values\n- dumps_csv(sheet: Sheet) -> str:\n  - serializes the sheet values to a CSV-formatted string using csv.writer over io.StringIO\n  - uses sheet.to_values(); preserves string cell semantics (values are written as-is)\n